<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Notifications' cross-section surface, carved into its own leaf project because most of
    its consumers are still in Base: eleven Humans.Application services emit through
    INotificationEmitter, AccountMergeService takes INotificationService (as does
    GoogleResourceReconciliationJob, from Humans.GoogleIntegration since the G5 jobs move),
    ConsentService and HumanLifecycleService auto-resolve through
    INotificationAutoResolve. A Contracts/ *folder* inside Humans.Notifications would
    therefore make Base reference a section and cycle (design §15.5b,
    memory/architecture/section-project-cycle-fix.md).

    INotificationRetention no longer counts towards that: its only consumer,
    CleanupNotificationsJob, moved into Humans.Notifications/Contracts/ at G5 lane 5b-1 once
    the "Hangfire pins a job to its assembly" claim was re-measured and found false
    (AddOrUpdate<T>(id, …) rewrites the stored type string at every startup, so the job id is
    the stable key). It could follow the job inward; that is a later pass.

    It holds exactly what lives outside the section: the four dispatch/auto-resolve/retention
    interfaces, and the three enums those signatures name — NotificationSource,
    NotificationClass and NotificationPriority cannot follow the entities into the section's
    internal Domain/ because every emit call site outside the section names them
    (design §15 step 5, Budget's rule). The inbox read models, the meter surface, the
    recipient resolver and the repository have no consumer outside the section and stay
    internal.

    References Humans.Interfaces only — the bottom of the graph.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Humans.Interfaces\Humans.Interfaces.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
